From 6d31ddd8973333e95ae7e3584e84029533064d69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zhang Qiao <zhangqiao22@huawei.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 11:05:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] scx_lavd: validate cached CPU pick against cpus_ptr on REENQ
 path

Task stall observed during scx_lavd testing:

  R k3s-server[8417] *root -21185ms
      scx_state/flags=4/0x3 dsq_flags=0x1 ops_state/qseq=0/0
      sticky/holding_cpu=-1/-1 dsq_id=0x5b
      dsq_vtime=45425777259 slice=4981350 weight=100
      cpus=00000000,00000000,00000000,00000000,00000000,00000000,00000000,00000000,00000020,00000000,00000000,00000000 no_mig=1
      \_ cpdom_id: 11   scpu: 91   cgroup: unknown[1303]

no_mig=1 means migration_disabled with cpus_ptr narrowed, but
suggested_cpu_id (scpu=91) is not in cpus_ptr. The task was routed
to the wrong per-CPU DSQ and starved.

Root cause: the REENQ path reuses the cached suggested_cpu_id
without checking cpus_ptr. A REENQ arrives at ops.enqueue()
directly without going through select_task_rq(), so the cache is
only refreshed by a later non-REENQ enqueue. Meanwhile, cpus_ptr
can change underneath:

  - migrate_disable() narrows cpus_ptr to the CPU the task is
    currently running on, which may differ from the cached one
    (the observed case: the task was consumed by another CPU via
    a shared cpdom DSQ, then called migrate_disable() there)
  - sched_setaffinity() or cgroup migration changes the task's
    affinity mask; if task_cpu is still allowed, the task is not
    re-enqueued, leaving the cache stale
  - CPU hotplug removes an offline CPU from cpus_ptr

Fix: clamp suggested_cpu_id to cpus_ptr, falling back to the
first allowed CPU. Mirrors existing validation in enqueue_cb().

Signed-off-by: Zhang Qiao <zhangqiao22@huawei.com>
---
 scheds/rust/scx_lavd/src/bpf/main.bpf.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/scheds/rust/scx_lavd/src/bpf/main.bpf.c b/scheds/rust/scx_lavd/src/bpf/main.bpf.c
index fa99d395ac..2bf4c1e648 100644
--- a/scheds/rust/scx_lavd/src/bpf/main.bpf.c
+++ b/scheds/rust/scx_lavd/src/bpf/main.bpf.c
@@ -939,6 +939,27 @@ void BPF_STRUCT_OPS(lavd_enqueue, struct task_struct *p, u64 enq_flags)
 		 * cpdom DSQ, never the local DSQ it was just drained from.
 		 */
 		cpu = taskc->suggested_cpu_id;
+		/*
+		 * suggested_cpu_id may be stale. It was set by a previous
+		 * ops.select_cpu()/ops.enqueue(), but a REENQ arrives at
+		 * ops.enqueue() directly without going through select_task_rq(),
+		 * so the cache is only refreshed by a later non-REENQ enqueue.
+		 * Meanwhile, cpus_ptr can change underneath:
+		 *   - migrate_disable() narrows cpus_ptr to the CPU the
+		 *     task is currently running on, which may differ from
+		 *     the cached one
+		 *   - sched_setaffinity() or cgroup migration changes the
+		 *     task's affinity mask; if task_cpu is still allowed,
+		 *     the task is not re-enqueued, leaving the cache stale
+		 *   - CPU hotplug removes an offline CPU from cpus_ptr
+		 * Clamp to cpus_ptr to prevent routing the task to the
+		 * per-CPU DSQ of a CPU that cannot run it.
+		 */
+		if (cpu < 0 || cpu >= nr_cpu_ids ||
+		    !bpf_cpumask_test_cpu(cpu, p->cpus_ptr)) {
+			cpu = bpf_cpumask_first(p->cpus_ptr);
+			taskc->suggested_cpu_id = cpu;
+		}
 		cpuc = get_cpu_ctx_id(cpu);
 		if (!cpuc) {
 			scx_bpf_error("Failed to lookup cpu_ctx %d", cpu);
